# n − d taken from the two filed totals, with the bounds crossed. One row per layer that states
# both a demand and a nameplate, so both operands exist. The key is (filing, layer).
#
# The operands are one relation's pair
#   layers/figures.sqlc sets each layer's demand beside its nameplate; this keeps the rows where
#   both are present.
#
# Subtracting two intervals reverses the bounds
#   The low of n − d pairs n's low against d's high, because the smallest remainder is the
#   smallest supply meeting the largest demand. Get it backwards and every remainder in the corpus
#   comes out inside out while every individual number still looks plausible.
#
# A bound, not the remainder, on a composed layer
#   The crossing assumes the two operands are independent, which on a composed layer they need not
#   be. Where one conversion factor multiplied both the nameplate and the demand of a part, pairing
#   `n_low` against `d_high` counts that factor's spread twice and the result is wider than the
#   remainder. This relation is therefore a bound on such a layer and not its remainder;
#   `composition/fused_remainders.sqlc` computes the remainder by converting each part's own, and
#   `layers/remainder.sqlc` is the one that chooses between them.
#
# Every other layer agrees under both readings
#   A point factor or no factor leaves nothing to decorrelate. Observation 13 prints the pair and
#   the agreeing rows are the control.
#
# The filed sign beside the derived one
#   The sign and the absorber are what the filing says, from layers/filed_remainders.sqlc. The join
#   is a LEFT JOIN because a layer may deny having a remainder and still state both operands; the
#   schema empties every part of a denied remainder, so such a layer carries the difference with
#   nothing filed beside it.
-- layers/figures.sqlc where both figures are present, beside the sign and absorber of
-- layers/filed_remainders.sqlc.
SELECT x.filing, x.layer,
       f.sign, f.sign_absent,
       f.absorber_taxonomy, f.absorber_value, f.absorber_absent,
       x.d_low, x.d_mode, x.d_high, x.d_unit AS unit,
       x.n_low, x.n_mode, x.n_high, x.n_unit AS amount_unit,
       x.n_low  - x.d_high AS r_low,   -- crossed: the low of n − d pairs n.low with d.high
       x.n_mode - x.d_mode AS r_mode,
       x.n_high - x.d_low  AS r_high,
       CASE WHEN x.n_low  - x.d_high >= 0 THEN 'clearance'::pm.fit
            WHEN x.n_high - x.d_low  <= 0 THEN 'interference'::pm.fit
            ELSE 'transition'::pm.fit END AS derived_fit,
       greatest(x.d_high - x.n_low, 0) AS exposure,
       x.lumpy, x.quantum_mode, x.quantum_unit
FROM      (
    :compose(layers/figures.sqlc)
) x
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(layers/filed_remainders.sqlc)
) f USING (filing, layer)
WHERE x.d_low IS NOT NULL
  AND x.n_low IS NOT NULL
